Why American cities pay over $3,000 for one trash can

High-tech, designer trash bins are popular with local governments. But many=
 people just want a regular can on the corner.

BY MARK DENT (https://x.com/mdent05?_hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz-8VLCyvaZG4ipyJJfNqU64Y=
yjXH5C3ejzsG4KEbmse2pzvaImt9GdSjAGFmUMSvOzrxj5lZdA64jHJwdcSXap_2ylEnrQ&_hsm=
i=3D401329326 )

High-tech, designer trash bins are popular with local governments. But many=
 people just want a regular can on the corner.

When Danny Sauter campaigned door-to-door in northeastern San Francisco in =
2024, he expected that residents would be united over concerns about homele=
ssness and housing costs. But a surprising issue kept popping up: trash can=
s.

Though San Francisco has ~3k of them on its infamously grimy sidewalks, ple=
nty of residents felt like there weren=E2=80=99t enough.

=E2=80=9CPeople would say, =E2=80=98I can walk 10 blocks without seeing a t=
rash can,=E2=80=99 or =E2=80=98there used to be a trash can on this corner =
for five years, and then it disappeared overnight,=E2=80=99=E2=80=9D says S=
auter, who won election as District 3 supervisor.

Early last year, he requested that the city put 1.5k more cans in his distr=
ict. He knows it won=E2=80=99t solve everything but believes a strategy of =
=E2=80=9Cmore trash cans and more pickups=E2=80=9D would create a cleaner e=
nvironment.

Public trash cans are a messy problem for American cities. Some municipalit=
ies believe that having fewer trash cans reduces litter. And some spend upw=
ards of $3k for a single trash can, believing premium designs and technolog=
y will solve their problems, only for residents to report similar cleanline=
ss issues as before.

What would seem to be a simple concept =E2=80=94 placing enough bins in wal=
kable, trafficked areas and servicing them frequently =E2=80=94 is a fraugh=
t endeavor, one that feeds into the growing belief (https://www.pewresearch=
.org/short-reads/2024/04/11/americans-rate-their-federal-state-and-local-go=
vernments-less-positively-than-a-few-years-ago/?_hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz-8VLCyvaZG4=
ipyJJfNqU64YyjXH5C3ejzsG4KEbmse2pzvaImt9GdSjAGFmUMSvOzrxj5lZdA64jHJwdcSXap_=
2ylEnrQ&_hsmi=3D401329326 ) that the government can=E2=80=99t get things do=
ne.

Why are public trash cans so complicated and expensive?

Walt Disney, Tokyo, and Gavin Newsom

Start a conversation with somebody who is interested in trash, and they=E2=
=80=99re likely to bring up one of two places where there practically isn=
=E2=80=99t any: Disney World or Tokyo.

Legend has it that Walt Disney researched customer behavior at amusement pa=
rks and concluded that deploying trash cans every 30 feet discouraged litte=
ring, leading to an abundance of trash cans on Disney properties. The Magic=
 Kingdom has an underground trash compacting system (https://www.waste360.c=
om/waste-reduction/a-look-at-walt-disney-world-s-underground-trash-tubes?_h=
senc=3Dp2ANqtz-8VLCyvaZG4ipyJJfNqU64YyjXH5C3ejzsG4KEbmse2pzvaImt9GdSjAGFmUM=
SvOzrxj5lZdA64jHJwdcSXap_2ylEnrQ&_hsmi=3D401329326 ) , and Travel + Leisure=
 once reported (https://www.mentalfloss.com/entertainment/disney/litter-dis=
ney-world?_hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz-8VLCyvaZG4ipyJJfNqU64YyjXH5C3ejzsG4KEbmse2pzvaIm=
t9GdSjAGFmUMSvOzrxj5lZdA64jHJwdcSXap_2ylEnrQ&_hsmi=3D401329326 ) that all e=
mployees, from custodians to Cinderella actresses, are required to pick up =
any piece of litter they come upon.

Walt Disney World=E2=80=99s Magic Kingdom. Notice the two trash cans close =
to each other on the left. (Gary Hershorn/Getty Images)

Tokyo, relative to America=E2=80=99s large cities, is spotless, too. But un=
like Disney World it has very few public trash cans, a policy resulting, in=
 part, from a doomsday cult using sarin in plastic bags during a 1995 attac=
k (https://psmag.com/environment/trash-cans-are-coming-back-to-japan/?_hsen=
c=3Dp2ANqtz-8VLCyvaZG4ipyJJfNqU64YyjXH5C3ejzsG4KEbmse2pzvaImt9GdSjAGFmUMSvO=
zrxj5lZdA64jHJwdcSXap_2ylEnrQ&_hsmi=3D401329326 ) .

=E2=80=9CI have colleagues here at Public Works who go on vacation in Japan=
 and, to the T, every one of them who's come back the first thing they say =
is not, =E2=80=98I just saw beautiful mountains or went to a tea house,=E2=
=80=99=E2=80=9D says Rachel Gordon, director of policy and communications f=
or the San Francisco Public Works department. =E2=80=9CIt's like, =E2=80=98=
Oh my God, there's no litter on the street, and there are no garbage cans.=
=E2=80=99=E2=80=9D

Nineteen years ago in San Francisco, then-mayor Gavin Newsom took a page fr=
om the Tokyo playbook. As part of an anti-litter initiative, San Francisco =
started removing public trash cans, a few hundred at first but eventually m=
ore than 33% of the city=E2=80=99s total, bringing its number from ~5k to ~=
3k.

Residents complained, but Newsom insisted (https://www.newspapers.com/image=
/462389548/?match=3D1&terms=3D%22san%20francisco%22%20and%20%22trash%20can%=
22%20and%20%22newsom%22&_hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz-8VLCyvaZG4ipyJJfNqU64YyjXH5C3ejzsG=
4KEbmse2pzvaImt9GdSjAGFmUMSvOzrxj5lZdA64jHJwdcSXap_2ylEnrQ&_hsmi=3D40132932=
6 ) that having a large quantity of trash cans at regular intervals led to =
illegal dumping by households and businesses. Fewer trash cans would discou=
rage this practice, he believed, and allow more space for pedestrians with =
handheld trash.

Resident satisfaction with the city=E2=80=99s cleanliness has stayed roughl=
y the same (https://sfcontroller.org/sites/default/files/FileCenter/Documen=
ts/6652-2015%20City%20Survey_final.pdf?_hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz-8VLCyvaZG4ipyJJfNqU=
64YyjXH5C3ejzsG4KEbmse2pzvaImt9GdSjAGFmUMSvOzrxj5lZdA64jHJwdcSXap_2ylEnrQ&_=
hsmi=3D401329326 ) in surveys since Newsom=E2=80=99s trash can cull, althou=
gh with grades in the C+ range, San Francisco isn=E2=80=99t earning any com=
parisons to Tokyo.

Gavin Newsom=E2=80=99s mayoral administration led an initiative to reduce t=
he number of San Francisco=E2=80=99s trash cans. (Michael Macor/The San Fra=
ncisco Chronicle via Getty Images)

Other cities have discovered that removing trash cans made things worse.

- After the New York City Transit Authority removed bins at a few dozen sta=
tions from 2011 to 2014, an audit found (https://www.osc.ny.gov/files/audit=
s/2018-01/SGA-2015-14s29.pdf?_hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz-8VLCyvaZG4ipyJJfNqU64YyjXH5C3=
ejzsG4KEbmse2pzvaImt9GdSjAGFmUMSvOzrxj5lZdA64jHJwdcSXap_2ylEnrQ&_hsmi=3D401=
329326 ) that at stations where receptacles were removed the average amount=
 of bags needed for collecting litter increased 3.2% and average track fire=
s increased 33%.
- Philadelphia tested out (https://www.phila.gov/media/20210312102943/ZWI-t=
rash-receptacle-placement-study-2017.pdf?_hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz-8VLCyvaZG4ipyJJfN=
qU64YyjXH5C3ejzsG4KEbmse2pzvaImt9GdSjAGFmUMSvOzrxj5lZdA64jHJwdcSXap_2ylEnrQ=
&_hsmi=3D401329326 ) various numbers of trash receptacles at select parks a=
nd commercial corridors, measuring the amount of waste, litter, and staff h=
ours needed for pickup. It found that more trash cans correlated with less =
litter and fewer trash cans correlated with more litter. (The Philadelphia =
Citizen opined on the discovery with the headline =E2=80=9CWater is Wet.=E2=
=80=9D (https://thephiladelphiacitizen.org/water-is-wet/?_hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz-8=
VLCyvaZG4ipyJJfNqU64YyjXH5C3ejzsG4KEbmse2pzvaImt9GdSjAGFmUMSvOzrxj5lZdA64jH=
JwdcSXap_2ylEnrQ&_hsmi=3D401329326 ) )

Syon Bhanot, a Swarthmore University behavioral and public economist who he=
lped with the research in Philadelphia, says the city ran the experiment be=
cause, as in San Francisco, significant amounts of litter in Philly come fr=
om =E2=80=9Cshort dumping,=E2=80=9D when people illegally unload garbage in=
 public areas.

Bhanot says social norms can influence behavior around waste and litter. Fo=
r instance, if people expect consistent curbside trash pickup, they might h=
ave less incentive to illegally dump a bag next to a public trash receptacl=
e in the middle of the week, reducing the need for receptacles. (Since 2024=
, Philadelphia has employed (https://whyy.org/articles/twice-weekly-trash-c=
ollection-expansion-philadelphia/?_hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz-8VLCyvaZG4ipyJJfNqU64Yyj=
XH5C3ejzsG4KEbmse2pzvaImt9GdSjAGFmUMSvOzrxj5lZdA64jHJwdcSXap_2ylEnrQ&_hsmi=
=3D401329326 ) twice-weekly trash pickups in several neighborhoods.)

The incentive to litter also comes down to a cultural expectation about wha=
t others condone, Bhanot says.

And Tokyo-like expectations don=E2=80=99t exist in most American cities. =
=E2=80=9CI=E2=80=99d like us to be a bit more of a tidy, polite culture, bu=
t we=E2=80=99re not there yet,=E2=80=9D Sauter says. That=E2=80=99s why he=
=E2=80=99d like San Francisco to add more trash cans.

While San Francisco has more bins per capita than New York City and less-de=
nse California cities like San Jose, it lags behind Washington, DC, which h=
as ~6.6k total trash cans and is often considered one of America=E2=80=99s =
cleanest cities.

The Hustle

Portland, Oregon, facing similar complaints (https://www.kgw.com/article/ne=
ws/local/portlands-cleaning-more-trash-of-the-streets-than-ever-but-you-wou=
ldnt-know-it/283-11640c78-e3d7-4af1-98ce-ae882c72c931?_hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz-8VLC=
yvaZG4ipyJJfNqU64YyjXH5C3ejzsG4KEbmse2pzvaImt9GdSjAGFmUMSvOzrxj5lZdA64jHJwd=
cSXap_2ylEnrQ&_hsmi=3D401329326 ) as San Francisco, nearly doubled (https:/=
/www.kgw.com/article/news/local/city-adds-public-trash-cans-nw-portland/283=
-15f93188-f456-449f-901b-bcd1042a46f9?_hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz-8VLCyvaZG4ipyJJfNqU6=
4YyjXH5C3ejzsG4KEbmse2pzvaImt9GdSjAGFmUMSvOzrxj5lZdA64jHJwdcSXap_2ylEnrQ&_h=
smi=3D401329326 ) its trash can total from 2016 to 2025, from 750 to ~1.4k,=
 focusing on neighborhoods outside the central city. It expects to have ~1.=
7k bins when the expansion is complete.

San Francisco, for now, is keeping its number of trash cans roughly the sam=
e while introducing a new model.

Paying a trash can premium

In December 2022, after years of expensive development, testing, public fee=
dback, and the fraud conviction of a former Public Works director, San Fran=
cisco announced it had settled on a trash can of the future to replace its =
aging fleet: the Slim Silhouette.

A stainless steel container with circular openings on its front side for ga=
rbage and recycling, it beat out cutting-edge designs such as the Bear Save=
r and Salt & Pepper (https://sfpublicworks.org/trashcanredesign?_hsenc=3Dp2=
ANqtz-8VLCyvaZG4ipyJJfNqU64YyjXH5C3ejzsG4KEbmse2pzvaImt9GdSjAGFmUMSvOzrxj5l=
ZdA64jHJwdcSXap_2ylEnrQ&_hsmi=3D401329326 ) . The city paid ~$19k to build =
the prototype and reached a deal with a contractor to manufacture the trash=
 cans for ~$1.4k each. That doesn=E2=80=99t include installation costs or s=
ensors that will provide an alert when the bins are full.

A Slim Silhouette rendering. (San Francisco Public Works)

It=E2=80=99s not an unusual price. Portland=E2=80=99s new trash cans cost ~=
$2k each. Washington, DC=E2=80=99s are ~1.5k. New York City has started rep=
lacing its longtime green mesh receptacles with $1.1k designer Better Bins =
(https://www.nyc.gov/site/dsny/collection/containerization/litter-basket-of=
-the-future.page?_hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz-8VLCyvaZG4ipyJJfNqU64YyjXH5C3ejzsG4KEbmse=
2pzvaImt9GdSjAGFmUMSvOzrxj5lZdA64jHJwdcSXap_2ylEnrQ&_hsmi=3D401329326 ) tha=
t cost more than 2x as much.

New York City=E2=80=99s Sanitation Department claims the Better Bins are le=
akproof and rat-resistant. A department spokesperson says they=E2=80=99ll l=
ast on the streets longer than the green mesh cans.

San Francisco tested out a generic $630 wire mesh trash can but chose the S=
lim Silhouette because it found the Silhouette=E2=80=99s opening, with a sn=
orkel design, was better at preventing rummaging and its stainless steel bo=
dy was easier to maintain. (It also costs less than the current models in u=
se by the Public Works Department.)

The eye-popping price of these modern trash cans has little to do with perm=
itting and red tape, like stories of $1.7m public toilets (https://thehustl=
e.co/the-fight-to-build-more-public-bathrooms-in-america?_hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz-8=
VLCyvaZG4ipyJJfNqU64YyjXH5C3ejzsG4KEbmse2pzvaImt9GdSjAGFmUMSvOzrxj5lZdA64jH=
JwdcSXap_2ylEnrQ&_hsmi=3D401329326 ) . Thoughtful designs are more expensiv=
e, and many cities believe it=E2=80=99s necessary to pay the premium.

=E2=80=9CIf we could use the $600 baskets, if they did what we needed them =
to do=E2=80=A6 and didn=E2=80=99t become more of a nuisance, we would have =
bought those,=E2=80=9D Gordon says. =E2=80=9CThat=E2=80=99s what we were ho=
ping would work.=E2=80=9D

She adds, =E2=80=9CThere=E2=80=99s never going to be a perfect garbage can.=
=E2=80=9D

The Hustle

Yet premium models don=E2=80=99t always accomplish the goals sought by the =
cities that buy them.

Several years ago, Tacoma, Washington, tested out smart trash cans made by =
Bigbelly, a popular brand that offers solar energy-powered bins with a comp=
acting feature, software connection, and a sensor that goes off when bins a=
re full. Besides the high upfront cost, the city dealt with software fees a=
nd solar panel theft and found the compaction didn=E2=80=99t reduce service=
 costs.

Tacoma has replaced the smart trash cans with non-compacting models by Bigb=
elly and Storr that cost less than half as much.

=E2=80=9CUltimately, we moved away from solar compaction because the math s=
imply didn't add up for our specific needs,=E2=80=9D says city spokesperson=
 Maria Lee, over email. =E2=80=9CWe found that a simple, fully enclosed can=
 prevents litter just as effectively.=E2=80=9D

Since Philadelphia went premium in the late 2000s and early 2010s, its tras=
h cans have been a point of contention among residents and politicians.

As part of an effort to reduce trash service expenses, the city=E2=80=99s S=
anitation Department installed ~1.1k compacting BigBelly trash cans. They c=
ost ~$3.7k each and replaced standard $100 wire baskets, according to the P=
hiladelphia City Controller (https://controller.phila.gov/wp-content/upload=
s/2018/06/BigbellyReport_June2017.pdf?_hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz-8VLCyvaZG4ipyJJfNqU6=
4YyjXH5C3ejzsG4KEbmse2pzvaImt9GdSjAGFmUMSvOzrxj5lZdA64jHJwdcSXap_2ylEnrQ&_h=
smi=3D401329326 ) . Because of anticipated lower service costs, the city ex=
pected savings of $13m over 10 years compared to the wire baskets.

The Hustle

The Controller=E2=80=99s Office found that the city never conducted a cost-=
benefit analysis to verify any savings. A former director of Philadelphia=
